Denormalization — trading redundancy for speed.

Normalization removed redundancy to make writes safe. But we saw the price: every question now needs joins. And for a warehouse, where a single report might scan millions of rows and join a dozen tables, that price is steep. So we deliberately turn the dial back.

Denormalization is the intentional adding of redundancy — merging tables, or duplicating columns — to improve read performance by reducing the number of joins. This isn't sloppy design. It's a considered trade, made exactly where fast queries matter more than compact storage.

Why is it the right call for a warehouse? Because reads dominate. The warehouse is loaded in controlled batches and then queried constantly, so optimizing the read is optimizing the common case. Fewer joins means the engine reads one wide table instead of stitching several together. And the queries themselves get simpler — analysts and B-I tools hit a flat, readable structure.

This is exactly why a dimension table in a star schema is denormalized. Instead of product, subcategory, category, and department spread across four normalized tables, all of it collapses into one flat product-dimension row. A single join from the fact table reaches every level of the hierarchy.

What do you give up? The redundancy that normalization fought comes back — so its old risks come with it, just managed rather than avoided. You use more storage, because data is duplicated. And a changed value has to be rewritten everywhere it was copied. In a warehouse that's acceptable, because updates arrive through a controlled E-T-L load, not through ad-hoc writes — the pipeline keeps the copies consistent.

So here's the rule of thumb. Normalize for writes, denormalize for reads. Operational systems in third normal form; warehouse dimensions flattened for speed. With that trade understood, the rest of the module turns to the keys that make both models work.
